 thousands of lives\n- 🏗️ **Infrastructure Planning** - Building resilient coastal communities\n- 📊 **Risk Assessment** - Identifying high-risk zones\n- 🔬 **Scientific Understanding** - Advancing seismology research\n\n### Key Statistics:\n- 💔 **2004 Indian Ocean Tsunami**: 230,000+ deaths across 14 countries\n- 💔 **2011 Japan Tsunami**: 18,000+ casualties, Fukushima disaster\n- 💔 **Annual Impact**: Earthquakes affect millions worldwide\n- 🌊 **Tsunami Risk**: ~24% of world's population lives in coastal zones\n\n### What Triggers a Tsunami?\n\nNot all earthquakes cause tsunamis. Key factors include:\n\n1. **Magnitude**: Typically requires M > 6.5\n2. **Depth**: Shallow earthquakes (<70km) are more dangerous\n3. **Location**: Underwater or near-coast seismic events\n4. **Fault Type**: Thrust/reverse faults displace water vertically\n5. **Intensity**: Strong ground shaking (MMI > 6)\n\n### Dataset Overview\n\nThis analysis uses **782 seismic records** with comprehensive features to predict tsunami occurrence:\n\n#### Features Available:\n- **Seismic Measurements**: Magnitude, Depth, Intensity (CDI, MMI)\n- **Station Data**: Number of stations (NST), Distance (DMIN), Gap\n- **Geographic**: Latitude, Longitude\n- **Temporal**: Year, Month\n- **Significance**: Overall impact measure (SIG)\n- **Target**: Tsunami occurrence (Binary: 1 = Yes, 0 = No)\n\n### Analysis Objectives\n\nThis comprehensive study aims to:\n\n1. 🔍 **Understand** the characteristics of tsunami-generating earthquakes\n2. 📊 **Visualize** geographic and temporal patterns in seismic activity\n3. 🧪 **Identify** key predictors of tsunami occurrence\n4. 🤖 **Build** accurate machine learning models for tsunami prediction\n5. 💡 **Provide** actionable insights for disaster preparedness\n\n### Why This Matters\n\n**Accurate tsunami prediction can:**\n- ✅ Enable faster evacuations\n- ✅ Reduce casualties and economic losses\n- ✅ Inform coastal development policies\n- ✅ Improve early warning systems\n- ✅ Support dis